Two related overlap bugs occur at the left wall: inserting a word to the left of a block at `RAIL_PADDING` clamps both to the same x, and a repelled block against the left wall has nowhere to go and overlaps its neighbor.

## Changes

### `snapToRail` — forward-sweep on left-insert ("train push")
- Removed `Math.max(RAIL_PADDING, ...)` clamp from the `result.side === 'right'` branch so `wordData.x` can go temporarily negative.
- Added a forward-sweep after x assignment that walks `rail.words` in array order and pushes each word right if it would overlap the previous one:

```js
let currentMinX = RAIL_PADDING;
rail.words.forEach(w => {
  if (w.x < currentMinX) { w.x = currentMinX; }
  const wEl = document.getElementById(`block-${w.id}`);
  const wWidth = wEl ? (wEl.offsetWidth || estimateBlockWidth(w.displayText || w.text))
                     : estimateBlockWidth(w.displayText || w.text);
  currentMinX = w.x + wWidth + BLOCK_GAP;
});
```

### `shakeKickFromRail` — reverse kick on wall collision ("jump over")
- Before applying the kick, checks whether a leftward kick would hit the boundary and reverses direction if so:

```js
if (kickDir === -1 && (wordData.x - (SNAP_DISTANCE + 10) < RAIL_PADDING)) {
  kickDir = 1; // wall hit — jump over the cluster instead
}
```

Continuation of the Android black-screen ANR work. Sweep of leftovers from the prior session uncovered a build break and a timer disposal race introduced by the new `System.Threading.Timer`-based safety nets.

### `LogManagement.cs` — missing `}` in `tryDropSentinel`
The newly added helper was unterminated, parsing `resolveStorageRoot` as a nested local function with an invalid `private` modifier (CS1513/CS0106). Would have failed the APK build on this branch.

```csharp
catch (Exception e)
{
    Debug.WriteLine(...);
}
                          // ← missing }
private static string? resolveStorageRoot() { ... }
```

### `OsuGameAndroid.cs` — double-dispose race on `coldStartTamingTimer` / `clearStartupSentinelTimer`
Both timers self-dispose from inside their own callback while `Dispose()` does the same on activity teardown. The previous pattern (`timer?.Dispose(); timer = null;`) is not atomic — a teardown racing the final tick could call `Dispose()` twice on the same handle. Switched all three sites to claim ownership via `Interlocked.Exchange` before disposing:

```csharp
var t = Interlocked.Exchange(ref coldStartTamingTimer, null);
try { t?.Dispose(); }
catch { /* ignore */ }
```

Applies to both timer-callback exit paths and the `Dispose(bool)` cleanup block.

